narrative:
## Type-Level Markers with PhantomData

`PhantomData[T]` marks that a struct logically owns or references type `T`, even though it doesn't store a `T` directly. It's zero-sized — no runtime cost.

### Why PhantomData?

```xiom
// Problem: this struct doesn't actually contain T
pub type Wrapper[T] = { ptr: *const Unit; }
// Compiler doesn't know Wrapper relates to T
// Drop check, variance, and auto-traits may be wrong

// Solution: add PhantomData
pub type Wrapper[T] = {
  ptr: *const Unit;
  _marker: PhantomData[T];
}
// Now compiler knows Wrapper owns a T
```

### Common Uses

```xiom
// 1. Indicating ownership without storing the value
pub type RawVec[T] = {
  ptr: *mut T;
  cap: Int;
  _own: PhantomData[T];  // marks that we logically own T's
}

// 2. Controlling variance
pub type Invariant[T] = {
  _marker: PhantomData[T];
}

// 3. Marking borrowing relationships
pub type BorrowedRef<'a, T> = {
  ptr: *const T;
  _lifetime: PhantomData<&'a T>;
}
```

### Zero-Sized

PhantomData takes no space:

```xiom
pub type WithPhantom[T] = {
  value: Int;
  _marker: PhantomData[T];
}
// size_of[WithPhantom] == size_of[Int]
// PhantomData adds zero bytes
```

### Drop Check

PhantomData influences drop check — the compiler knows when T should be dropped:

```xiom
pub type MyBox[T] = {
  ptr: *mut T;
  _marker: PhantomData[T];  // MyBox owns T, drop should free it
}
```

